{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "bs-01-downstream-upstream-core-speeds",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Downstream, Upstream, Still-Water Speed, and Current",
    "description": "Build the core u + v / u − v river-speed model and recover boat or current speed from observed journeys.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with a moving-walkway picture: the current helps downstream and resists upstream. Name the two base speeds only after the learner sees the effect. Then reverse the model with half-sum and half-difference, hide the effective speeds inside distance/time data, and finish with a source-shaped capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 19, Boats and Streams. Approved scope: Teach downstream as boat speed in still water plus current, and upstream as boat speed in still water minus current. Use a moving-belt mental model so the signs make sense. Cover recovering still-water speed as half the sum of downstream and upstream speeds and current speed as half their difference, deriving observed speeds from distance and time, finding a missing upstream or downstream speed from the other quantities, and checking that upstream is slower than downstream. Include source-shaped questions where downstream and upstream speeds are hidden inside different distances and times."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the two speeds hiding inside every boat-and-stream question.\n\nA boat has its own speed. The river adds a push in one direction and takes the same push away in the other.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Picture a moving walkway.\n\nA boat moves at 12 km/hr in still water. The current flows at 3 km/hr.\n\nDownstream, both pushes point the same way: 12 + 3 = 15 km/hr.\nUpstream, the river pushes back: 12 − 3 = 9 km/hr.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let u be the boat's speed in still water and v the current.\n\nDownstream = u + v\nUpstream = u − v\n\nThe current is the same size in both directions; only its sign changes.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
